<p>We know BFS traverses nodes level by level. However, standard BFS doesn'=
t provide explicit markers for when one level ends and another begins. In c=
ontrast, there is a type of BFS traversal that allows us to process one lev=
el at a time. This algorithm is called level-order traversal.</p>
<p><strong>Level-order traversal</strong><br>
The core idea of level-order traversal is that at any level of the tree, th=
e children of the nodes at that level comprise the next level. This means t=
he children of level 1=E2=80=99s nodes make up level 2, and likewise, the c=
hildren of level 2=E2=80=99s nodes make up level 3, and so on. To see how t=
his works, consider the binary tree below, with the BFS queue initialized w=
